statement.paragraph:
(T/F) If $\textbf{u}$ and $\textbf{v}$ are in $\text{ℝ}^{2}$ and $\text{det }\left\lbrack \begin{array}{rr}
    \textbf{u} & \textbf{v}
    \end{array} \right\rbrack = 10$, then the area of the triangle in the plane with vertices at $\textbf{0}$, $\textbf{u}$, and $\textbf{v}$ is $10$.

paragraph.math:
$\left| \begin{array}{rrr}
    1 & a & {b + c} \\
    1 & b & {a + c} \\
    1 & c & {a + b}
    \end{array} \right|$

$\left| \begin{matrix}
    a & b & c \\
    {a + x} & {b + x} & {c + x} \\
    {a + y} & {b + y} & {c + y}
    \end{matrix} \right|$

statement.paragraph:
Show that the equation of the line in $\text{ℝ}^{2}$ through distinct points $\left( x_{1},\left. y_{1} \right) \right.$ and $\left( x_{2},\left. y_{2} \right) \right.$ can be written as $$\text{det~}\begin{bmatrix}
    1 & x_{1} & y \\
    1 & x_{1} & y_{1} \\
    1 & x_{2} & y_{2}
    \end{bmatrix}\text{~=~0}$$
